    "text": "{\"anomaly_detected\": true, \"confidence\": 0.8, \"anomalies\": [{\"category\": \"short_to_ground\", \"components\": [\"R3\", \"D2\"], \"pins\": [\"R3.1\", \"R3.2\", \"D2.1\", \"D2.2\"], \"nets\": [\"GND\", \"/Microcontroller/HEATER\"], \"observed\": \"Both pins of R3 (500 ohm) are on the GND net. The HEATER net contains only D2 pin 2 (anode), not R3. D2's cathode is on GND, so the series resistor is bypassed and the status LED sits directly between the heater-drive net and ground.\", \"expected\": \"Per the spec, R3 is a series resistor on the HEATER net, placed between the GPIO1/HEATER drive net and the D2 LED anode, so that R3 limits LED current.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"13\\\") (name \\\"GND\\\") ... (node (ref \\\"R3\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\")) (node (ref \\\"R3\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"4\\\") (name \\\"/Microcontroller/HEATER\\\") ... (node (ref \\\"D2\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"A\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"A status LED (`D2`) and series resistor (`R3`) are also on the `HEATER` net\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R3 0 0 500\"}], \"impact\": \"R3 is dead: it is shorted across ground and does not limit the LED current. The GPIO1 drive to D2 has no series resistance, which can overdrive the pin or the LED. The resistor's intended current limit is lost, though the heater switch path itself is unaffected.\", \"confidence\": 0.7}, {\"category\": \"extra_component\", \"components\": [\"J5\"], \"pins\": [\"J5.1\", \"J5.2\"], \"nets\": [\"/Microcontroller/HEATER\", \"GND\"], \"observed\": \"J5 is a 2-pin screw terminal on the Microcontroller sheet with pin 2 on the HEATER net (the ESP32 GPIO1 drive line, which also feeds U6 IN and DIAG_EN) and pin 1 on GND. It is not in the spec. The heater output connector is J6.\", \"expected\": \"The spec documents the heater output only through the TPS1H200 OUT to heater connector J6. It does not describe a second terminal wired to the raw GPIO1 control line.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"4\\\") (name \\\"/Microcontroller/HEATER\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"J5\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"Pin_2\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"`OUT` (pin 7) feeds the heater connector `J6` pin 2\"}], \"impact\": \"If J5 is not a deliberate test or debug header, the raw 3.3 V GPIO1 drive is exposed on an external terminal, which could be shorted or loaded from outside the board, and the heater control line would be exposed at an unexpected place.\", \"confidence\": 0.2}, {\"category\": \"wrong_value\", \"components\": [\"C7\"], \"pins\": [\"C7.1\", \"C7.2\"], \"nets\": [\"Net-(U1-EN{slash}CHIP_PU)\", \"GND\"], \"observed\": \"C7 is 10uF on the ESP32 EN net with R5 (10k) as the pull-up, giving a roughly 100 ms RC delay. The field is also oddly named \\\"LCSC`\\\", unlike the other parts. The spec does not mention C7.\", \"expected\": \"A typical ESP32-C3 EN reset RC is about 10k with 0.1 to 1 uF. A 10 uF value is unusual but not clearly wrong, and the spec is silent on it.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(comp (ref \\\"C7\\\") (value \\\"10uF\\\")\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"25\\\") (name \\\"Net-(U1-EN{slash}CHIP_PU)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"C7\\\") (pin \\\"1\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}], \"impact\": \"The EN power-on delay is much longer than typical. This is unlikely to prevent boot, so the impact is minor.\", \"confidence\": 0.12}]], \"summary\": \"The most likely anomaly is R3, the series resistor for the heater status LED D2. Both of its pins are on GND, so it is shorted and bypassed, contrary to the spec, which places it on the HEATER net. The other two findings (the extra J5 terminal on the GPIO1 line and the 10 uF EN cap C7) are lower-confidence observations.\"}",
